2026-10-31 11:43 Result: patch at scratchpad/w-core338/k338.patch (against HEAD 3d6e6e2, git apply --check clean; not applied to the shared tree). Files: lib/OCP/Corefile.pm (new), lib/OCP/Cmd/Apply/Network.pm, lib/OCP/Cmd/Apply.pm, lib/OCP/Drift.pm, t/338-registry-dns-one-reading.t (new).

Parser: new Moo class OCP::Corefile (text -> lines/depth/blocks; block_for picks the server block whose zone covers a name most specifically, root '.' as catch-all, first on a tie; record/record_index/address_of read the hosts record in that block). Not in Network.pm: Drift would have to `use OCP::Cmd::Apply::Network`, which already uses OCP::Drift (compile-time cycle; breaking it with a runtime require goes against getty-perl-core), and ocp status would load an apply command module. Not in Drift.pm: a parsed Corefile is state a Moo object holds, the grammar is neither drift nor apply, and it keeps the Drift.pm diff small ahead of k313. Network.pm's three depth loops and corefile_root_block are gone; corefile_has_root_block delegates (its POD stays with k337). Removed the uncalled forwarder Apply.pm::_corefile_root_block.

How the two sides agree now: the answering block maps the name to the CP -> writer 0 / probe undef. The answering block is root and the record is missing or stale -> writer patches / probe kind missing "ocp apply restores it" (same as before). Any other block answers wrongly, or no block answers -> writer dies naming the block / probe kind spec + manual_step (not counted fixable by Status/dry run). The writer still writes only into the root block (ADR 0016/0022).

Behaviour changes: no root block + own block right: die -> 0 (the card case). Own block stale or without a record: die with a message naming the shadowing block. Root + own block right: used to write a shadowed root record -> 0. Root right + own block stale: used to be 0 -> dies. Probe: a record outside the answering block no longer counts. A stale own block is reported with the address CoreDNS answers. Cases ocp apply cannot fix are kind spec instead of missing. The writer's record recognizer now needs a '.' or ':' in the address (the old regex took pure hex words such as `add` for an address).

